<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch7-chapter-CI-bootstrap" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Parameter Estimation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> 
      Students have been learning how important it is to consider sampling variability when making decisions about hypothesis tests. So far we have been able to measure this (under hypothetical conditions - null hypothesis) by simulating statistics using manipulatives, such as cards, coins, spinners, etc. This works well when our observed data is categorical, but we need different methods when data is quantitative.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here we introduce bootstrapping for the estimation of sampling variability. Students will learn how to simulate the random sampling process when we only have access to sample data and not the full population. This method is useful for hypothesis testing and also for parameter estimation, which is the main focus of this chapter. Students will learn to compute confidence intervals for one mean and for one proportion. 
    </p>
    
    <p>
      There will be a large emphasis interpretation, and we will pay attention to effect size and practical importance. Students will also learn the 
      effect of sample size and confidence level on the width of the confidence interval. There will be a discussion on precision and bias.
    </p>

  </introduction>


  <objectives>
    <ol>
    <li>Explore...</li>

    <li>Learn...</li>

    <li>Understand...</li>

    <li>Apply...</li>
  </ol>
  </objectives>

  

